==Spanish==

===Etymology===
{{inh+|es|osp|cozer}}, from {{inh|es|LL.|cocō|cocere}}, from {{inh|es|la|coquo|coquere}}. Cognate with {{cog|en|cook}}.

Some conjugated forms of the verb were reformed through analogy with the infinitive; in older Spanish, the forms {{m|es|cueza}} and {{m|es|cuezo}} were {{m|es|cuega}} and {{m|es|cuego}} (< {{cog|LL.||cocam}}, {{m|la||cocō}}), {{m|es|cocí}} was {{m|es|coxe}} (< {{cog|la|coxī}}), and the past participle was {{m|es|cocho}} (< {{cog|la|coctus}}).<ref>{{R:es:DCECH}}</ref>

===Pronunciation===
{{es-pr|+<audio:LL-Q1321 (spa)-AdrianAbdulBaha-cocer.wav<a:Colombia>><hmp:coser<a:Latin America>>}}

===Verb===
{{es-verb}}

# to [[boil]]
# to [[cook]] {{gl|only used in situations where the food being prepared is submitted to ''fire''; such as through the processes of boiling, simmering or steaming anything; or baking bread slowly in an oven}}
#: {{coi|es|cocer a fuego lento|[[simmer]]}}
# {{lb|es|reflexive|figurative}} to [[brew]]
#: {{ux|es|Algo se está '''cociendo'''|Something is '''brewing'''}}
# {{lb|es|pronominal}} to [[chafe]] {{gloss|get sore}}
#: {{syn|es|[[escocer]]se|[[escaldar]]se}}

